bench/ -- the token-benchmark epic's (#152) measurement tree
==============================================================

This directory holds the epic's deterministic and observed benchmark tiers
and the append-only ledger they all write to. It is established by issue
#323 (Tier 0 + the ledger) as the base a successor slice builds inside, so
the shapes below are load-bearing -- read this before adding a tier.

Design doc: docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-13-token-benchmark-design.md
(branch docs/token-benchmark-spec). That doc was not present on this
directory's base branch (main) at the time issue #323 was implemented; the
layout below follows the issue body's restated Tier-0 and ledger
requirements directly, not the design doc's own wording.

Layout
------

    bench/
      README             -- this file: layout + ledger schema
      tier0.sh            -- Tier 0: static resident/reachable/dispatched-
                              template token accounting at a git SHA, no
                              network, no model call
      results/
        tier0.jsonl       -- Tier 0's ledger records (one per bench/tier0.sh
                              invocation)
        <tierN>.jsonl      -- later tiers append their own records to their
                              own file; see "One file per tier" below

A later tier's script (bench/tierN.sh) is expected to follow the same shape:
a single executable script under bench/, appending JSON Lines records to its
own bench/results/*.jsonl file, sharing this same schema's key fields.

One file per tier, one ledger schema
-------------------------------------

Every bench/results/*.jsonl file uses the SAME record shape and the SAME
grouping key, so a chart or comparison script can treat the whole
bench/results/*.jsonl glob as one logical ledger without per-tier special
cases:

Grouping key: (plugin_sha, fixture_version, model, effort)

This key is NOT unique across appends -- it is a grouping/comparison key,
not a primary key. Running a bench/*.sh script twice for the same SHA (the
same measurement, taken again) appends a second row carrying the identical
key, distinguished only by `measured_at`; the append-only test in
tests/test-bench-tier0.sh deliberately re-measures the same SHA and asserts
exactly this. That is intentional: the property this ledger actually
guarantees is append-only (see below), not key uniqueness, and re-measuring
a SHA -- to confirm a number, or to compare an estimator change against a
frozen fixture -- is a normal thing to want to do without inventing a new
fixture_version for it. A consumer that needs one row per key reduces over
duplicates itself (latest `measured_at` wins is the natural default); a
consumer that wants every measurement reads them all. If a future tier
needs true uniqueness enforced by the ledger writer itself (reject-on-
duplicate, or require a bumped fixture_version), that is a deliberate
behavior change to make and document here, not the default.

- `plugin_sha`   -- full 40-character git commit SHA the measurement was
                    taken at (resolved via `git rev-parse --verify`, never a
                    short or ambiguous ref).
- `fixture_version` -- names the frozen fixture set (issue body, prior art,
                    write set, ...) a tier's measurement was taken against,
                    present from day one even though Tier 0 has no fixture
                    dependency of its own. Tier 0 records use `tier0-v1`.
                    A later tier bumps this string whenever its fixture set
                    changes in a way that would make old and new rows
                    incomparable.
- `model`        -- the model identifier a tier's run used. Tier 0 makes no
                    model call, so it uses the reserved sentinel
                    `static-accounting` rather than a real model id -- a
                    reader can tell a Tier-0 row apart from a real model run
                    without inspecting anything else.
- `effort`       -- the effort/reasoning level a tier's run used. Tier 0's
                    sentinel is `n/a` for the same reason.

Common fields on every record:

- `measured_at`  -- UTC ISO-8601 instant the record was appended (`--timestamp`
                    lets a test pin this for reproducibility).

Tier-0-specific fields (bench/tier0.sh):

- `resident`   -- `{bytes, tokens}` -- every `agentkit/skills/**/SKILL.md`
                  file's own size, summed. What a session pays for a skill
                  existing, before any reference is ever read.
- `reachable`  -- `{bytes, tokens}` -- every `*.md` file under
                  `agentkit/skills/` (SKILL.md + references/*.md +
                  .shared/*.md), summed. The ceiling if every reference were
                  read.
- `dispatched_template` -- `{bytes, tokens, present, source}` -- the static
                  size of `parallel-issues/references/worker-prompts.md`
                  alone: the one reference rendered and paid on every
                  dispatch (hit rate 1.0 by construction), per the
                  maintainer comment on issue #323. `present` is `false` for
                  a SHA before that file existed (e.g. `06d18cf`), in which
                  case `bytes`/`tokens` read 0 rather than being omitted --
                  a consumer can always read these fields without a
                  presence check first. This is the template-only,
                  fixture-free component of the full per-dispatch surface;
                  the per-issue substitution byte count (issue body, prior
                  art, write set) needs a frozen reference-dispatch fixture
                  and belongs to a later slice, not this one.

`tokens` fields are always `bytes / 4` (integer division), computed through
the single shared estimator in tests/lib/token-estimate.sh -- the same
function tests/lint-skill-size.sh's SKILL.md body-budget gate calls. Two
surfaces sharing one function/constant means they cannot silently disagree
about the same bytes; see that file's header comment.

Append-only
-----------

Every bench/*.sh script only ever appends a line to its ledger file --
never rewrites, reorders, or removes an existing line. This is what lets
downstream charting be a pure function of the ledger: replaying it from
scratch always reproduces the same chart, and a chart script never needs
to guess whether a row it read might later be overwritten out from under
it. tests/test-bench-tier0.sh asserts this property directly (a second
invocation strictly grows the file; every previously-written line is
byte-identical afterward).

CI recording coverage
----------------------

.github/workflows/record-tier0.yml appends a bench/results/tier0.jsonl row
for a merge to main by triggering on CI's own `workflow_run` completion
event, not on `push` directly. That makes the contract precise: **a record
exists for every merge whose CI run completed successfully.** It does NOT
exist for a merge with no successful CI completion -- whether CI ran and
failed, or CI never ran at all because something skipped it (including a
merge/squash commit whose message contains `[skip ci]`/`[ci skip]`, which
is ordinary user-writable text, not something this ledger controls).

A commit missing from this ledger is therefore not, by itself, evidence of
a recording bug. It means that commit was never independently verified
green by CI -- the same reason a commit with failing tests is already,
deliberately, absent. Treat gaps as expected for unverified merges; if you
need to confirm whether a specific gap is a bug or an unverified merge,
check that commit's CI run (or the absence of one) directly rather than
assuming the ledger should be complete.

Tier 1's task substrate -- Tally, the ten issues, and the gold tree
------------------------------------------------------------------

Issue #325 (slice 3 of the epic) adds the fixed, controlled task substrate
Tier 1 measures agents against. Like issue #323's Tier 0, the epic's design
doc (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-13-token-benchmark-design.md`, branch
`docs/token-benchmark-spec`) was NOT present on this directory's base branch
at implementation time. Everything below follows the issue #325 body's
restated structural requirements directly -- see "Construction decisions"
at the end of this stanza for the specific choices the absent doc would
otherwise have pinned down.

    bench/
      fixtures/tally/    -- the Tally skeleton: a small, dependency-free
                             ES-module SPA (index.html + src/store.js +
                             src/render.js + src/main.js), plus its own
                             public smoke test (test/smoke.mjs, runnable via
                             `node test/smoke.mjs`, no network, no bundler)
                             and a hand-authored .agent/config.env (see
                             below).
      issues/*.md         -- the ten frozen issue bodies, one file each,
                             spec-grade (a concrete, checkable Acceptance
                             Criteria list per issue). Each carries a small
                             structured frontmatter block
                             (id / conflict_group / files / blocked_by)
                             that verify-issues.py reads.
      gold/tally/          -- the reference solution: bench/fixtures/tally
                             with all ten issues' changes applied and
                             merged (the two render.js issues and the three
                             store.js issues coexist in one file each; the
                             five disjoint issues each add one standalone
                             module + its own test/*.smoke.mjs).
      build-gold.sh         -- builds (syntax-checks every source file,
                             confirms index.html's entry-point wiring) and
                             runs (every test/*.smoke.mjs) bench/gold/tally
                             standalone. `bench/build-gold.sh` with no
                             arguments.
      verify-issues.py      -- mechanically verifies bench/issues/*.md's
                             conflict/dependency structure: exactly ten
                             issues, exactly 2 sharing src/render.js and 3
                             sharing src/store.js (conflicting), exactly 5
                             touching no file any other issue touches
                             (disjoint), and a blocked_by DAG whose longest
                             chain is exactly depth 3. `python3
                             bench/verify-issues.py`, no arguments, no
                             network, stdlib only.
      verify-config-env.sh   -- proves
                             bench/fixtures/tally/.agent/config.env is
                             resolver-parseable at both benchmark-arm SHAs
                             (06d18cf, 53e7e8c): extracts
                             repo-config.sh as it existed at each SHA and
                             asserts every key the fixture's config.env
                             declares actually resolves through it, not
                             just that the resolver exits 0. `bench/
                             verify-config-env.sh`, no arguments.

The conflict/dependency structure, exactly as issue #325 fixed it:

    conflict_group=render (2): tally-01, tally-02        -- both edit
        src/render.js's header/list region in renderApp.
    conflict_group=store  (3): tally-03, tally-04, tally-05 -- all three
        add a new exported function to src/store.js.
    conflict_group=none   (5, disjoint): tally-06..tally-10 -- each adds
        exactly one standalone module (+ its own test file); no file is
        shared between any two of them, and none touches render.js or
        store.js.
    blocked_by chain: tally-06 -> tally-07 -> tally-08 -> tally-09 (three
        edges, depth 3 -- one under both arms' byte-identical chain depth
        cap of 4). tally-10 is a second, shorter branch off tally-06
        (depth 1) so the graph's longest path stays exactly 3.

Frozen-set boundary
--------------------

bench/fixtures/**, bench/gold/**, and bench/issues/** are this slice's
frozen set, per issue #325's "Freezing" requirement: a fixture whose exact
bytes later changed out from under a published Tier-1 result would make
that result silently incomparable to a re-run. Any future edit to a file
under those three trees -- fixing a bug in the gold solution, rewording an
issue's acceptance criteria, adjusting the base Tally skeleton -- forks
`fixture_version` to a new string (e.g. `tier1-v2`) rather than mutating
`tier1-v1` (or whatever a later Tier-1 ledger slice names it) in place.
This mirrors Tier 0's `fixture_version` convention above; enforcing it
mechanically (e.g. a hash-pin test) is left to the ledger slice that
actually writes Tier-1 rows, per the issue body.

bench/build-gold.sh and bench/verify-issues.py are tooling, not fixture
content -- they are expected to gain fixes/features without forking
`fixture_version`, the same way bench/tier0.sh itself is not part of Tier
0's frozen set.

Construction decisions (no design doc to defer to)
----------------------------------------------------

The design doc would have specified: Tally's exact feature list, and the
ten issues' exact wording. In its absence, this slice made these choices,
recorded here rather than silently implied as the design doc's own:

  - Tally is a tally-counter list app (add a named counter, +/-, remove),
    not any other app shape -- chosen only because it is small enough to
    hand-author ten spec-grade issues against without an oracle yet.
  - store.js and render.js are both plain pure functions (no framework, no
    real DOM in render.js -- it returns HTML strings) specifically so the
    base fixture and the gold tree are testable under plain `node`, with
    no jsdom/browser dependency and therefore no network install.
  - The five disjoint issues are each scoped to a single new standalone
    module that is NOT wired into src/main.js. This is what makes
    "disjoint" mean something real at the file level (no issue's
    acceptance criteria can be satisfied by touching src/main.js), at the
    cost of the resulting app not actually surfacing dark mode/keyboard
    shortcuts/export/import/stats in the UI -- wiring them in is
    explicitly out of scope for this fixture slice.
  - The blocked_by chain is built entirely from the disjoint issue set
    (tally-06..tally-10), not from the conflicting issues. The conflicting
    issues are deliberately independent of each other in the blocked_by
    graph -- the whole point of the render.js/store.js conflict pairs is
    that they are dispatched in parallel and collide, which a blocked_by
    edge would prevent from ever happening.
  - bench/fixtures/tally/.agent/config.env's key set (AGENT_REPO_SLUG,
    AGENT_BASE_BRANCH, AGENT_WORKTREE_ROOT, AGENT_BRANCH_PREFIXES,
    AGENT_LABEL_TYPES/AREAS/PRIORITIES, AGENT_CMD_TEST) was chosen by
    diffing agentkit/skills/.shared/scripts/repo-config.sh's ACCEPTED_KEYS
    array at 06d18cf and 53e7e8c directly (`git show <sha>:path | grep
    ACCEPTED_KEYS` at each) and keeping only the intersection -- not by
    trusting the issue body's claim of "byte-identical cap strings"
    verbatim. Every key used here is in that intersection at both SHAs.

Reproducing the epic's Tier-0 numbers
--------------------------------------

    bench/tier0.sh 06d18cf
    bench/tier0.sh 53e7e8c

reproduce the epic's already-known table -- resident -59.4%, reachable
-8.9%, both within rounding -- because both `06d18cf` and `53e7e8c` are real
commits in this repository's own history (the compression refactor that
split review-remote-pr and parallel-issues into dispatcher + references/).
Measured directly against this repo:

    06d18cf: resident 278317 B (~69579 tok)  reachable 278806 B (~69701 tok)
    53e7e8c: resident 112969 B (~28242 tok)  reachable 253969 B (~63492 tok)

    resident:  (69579 - 28242) / 69579 = -59.40%
    reachable: (69701 - 63492) / 69701 =  -8.91%

The Tier-1 trial harness (issue #327)
--------------------------------------

Issue #327 (slice 5 of the epic) adds the harness that actually RUNS a
Tier-1 trial: a pinned container, `bench/run-trial.sh` (lifecycle, repo
reset, invocation, oracle scoring, ledger append), and
`bench/parse-rollout.py` (turns a trial's Codex session logs into one
ledger row). Design doc sections "Environment" and "Instrumentation"
(`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-13-token-benchmark-design.md`, branch
`docs/token-benchmark-spec` -- again not present on this directory's base
branch at implementation time; see the two "Construction decisions"
stanzas above for the same situation on the prior two slices).

    bench/
      container/
        Dockerfile         -- the one image every trial container is built
                              from: debian:13 pinned by digest, every apt
                              package pinned by exact version, the Codex
                              CLI pinned by its release-asset sha256. See
                              the Dockerfile's own header comment for
                              exactly how each pin was resolved, and
                              bench/container/README.md for build/re-pin
                              instructions.
        README.md          -- building, re-pinning, and the secrets rule
                              (never baked into the image; see there).
      lib/
        concurrency-cap-gate.sh   -- the "present and identical on both
                              arms, or abort before spend" control, built
                              on top of the shipped
                              agentkit/skills/parallel-issues/scripts/
                              concurrency-cap.sh parser (reused, not
                              reimplemented -- see that file's own header
                              comment).
        home-empty.sh       -- builds and verifies the baked-empty home
                              directory the design doc calls "the control
                              most likely to silently destroy the study."
        container-lifecycle.sh -- build/run/destroy, and the destruction
                              PROOF the acceptance criteria ask for. Has a
                              dry-run mode that runs the same state machine
                              against plain marker files instead of a real
                              docker daemon -- see its header comment for
                              why that is the only mode this repo's tests
                              and CI ever exercise.
        rate-limit-gate.sh  -- "pause rather than fail mid-run" against the
                              GraphQL pool (`gh api rate_limit`), the same
                              pool this repo's own
                              github-api-budget rule calls the scarce one.
        ledger-append.sh    -- the append-only, symlink-refusing discipline
                              bench/tier0.sh keeps inline, extracted here so
                              bench/run-trial.sh's second ledger file
                              (results/tier1.jsonl) does not carry its own
                              divergent copy.
      run-trial.sh          -- one trial, wiring every lib/ piece together;
                              `--dry-run` (default) exercises the full
                              sequence with no docker, no Codex process, no
                              spend; `--live` is the real path, unexercised
                              by this repo's tests or CI. See its own header
                              comment.
      parse-rollout.py      -- one trial's `~/.codex/sessions/*.jsonl`-shaped
                              logs in, one Tier-1 ledger record out: the
                              ledger key, assigned vs. realised effort tier
                              (and the void-trial rule this makes
                              mechanical -- PREREGISTRATION.md's "Void
                              trials"), the four token classes separately
                              per actor (orchestrator vs. each worker),
                              blended USD, per-path reference-read counts
                              (the primary Tier-1 instrument), and the
                              orchestration-level facts (selected issues,
                              chain plan, serialization/retry events, wall
                              clock, exit condition) a harness-appended
                              `bench_trial_meta` record carries -- see that
                              script's own "Construction decisions" for why
                              those specific fields come from the harness
                              rather than from parsing LLM tool-call prose.

`bench/results/tier1.jsonl` is this harness's ledger file (same schema
family as `tier0.jsonl`: append-only, keyed by `(plugin_sha,
fixture_version, model, effort)` -- see "One file per tier, one ledger
schema" above). It is not committed by this slice: every test that exercises
`run-trial.sh` passes its own `--ledger` under a scratch directory, so the
real path stays absent until an actual trial runs -- see
tests/test-bench-accept.sh's "no bench/results/tier1.jsonl exists yet"
assertion, which this slice deliberately leaves true.

What is and is not exercised by this slice's tests
-----------------------------------------------------

`tests/test-bench-harness.sh` and `tests/test-bench-parse-rollout.sh` run
every gate, the container-lifecycle state machine, and the full
`run-trial.sh` sequence end to end in dry-run mode, against a synthetic
Codex-session fixture (`tests/fixtures/bench/sessions/*.jsonl`) built to a
schema documented in `bench/parse-rollout.py`'s own header comment. Real
Codex CLI rollout files are not available to read or generate from inside
this task's declared scope, and a real trial would spend real money -- both
explicitly out of scope for this slice (see the issue's acceptance
criteria). Two things are therefore left to the operator, unverified by
this slice:

  - `bench/container/Dockerfile` building successfully for real (never run
    by CI; see bench/container/README.md).
  - One real, paid trial end-to-end, which would also be the first chance
    to confirm bench/parse-rollout.py's assumed session-log schema against
    Codex's actual output rather than this slice's synthetic fixture.
